WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:07.860
That’s right, this is happening.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.640
OK, so why is this not just a steaming
pile of clickbait?

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:16.640
Hear me out.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.430
So far, there have been twenty of these movies.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:26.570
And uber-producer Kevin Feige says there’ll
be at least 20 more in the MCU — Marvel

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:30.460
Cinematic Universe — within the next 10
years.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:35.980
Seriously, half of these people could disappear
and it would still be 148 too many characters.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:42.640
When we asked Vox’s audience for things
they think are overrated, Marvel and DC’s

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:45.820
movies came up again and again.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:51.750
We love these movies - in the United States
alone, they’ve grossed more than 6.5 billion

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:52.480
dollars.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.660
And yet there’s something missing.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.460
When you look at the history of fictional
universes — and at the MCU in particular

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:03.420
— it’s possible to understand how it could
be fixed.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:16.039
Go back to 1939 and you’ll find the likely
birth of the Marvel Universe.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.280
It’s in Marvel Mystery Comics #7.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.670
These characters aren’t that well-known
today.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:29.060
But here, Betty tells Namor that The Human
Torch exists — establishing continuity and

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.579
crossover potential.

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:35.069
This was the start of a new era — one in
which corporations produced fiction.

00:01:35.069 --> 00:01:39.549
The idea was simple — you could use the fame
of one property to enhance the audience for

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:40.549
another.

00:01:40.549 --> 00:01:45.389
And Marvel wasn’t alone in recognizing the
financial opportunity of crossovers in the

00:01:45.389 --> 00:01:47.120
1930s and 40s.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.420
“The Green Hornet!

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:54.660
Look at that picture on the wall.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.700
The man on that horse is one of your ancestors.”

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:03.279
In the 1940s, The Green Hornet revealed that
the series’ central character, Britt Reid,

00:02:03.279 --> 00:02:08.770
was the grandnephew of The Lone Ranger, another
popular radio star at the time.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:14.909
“I hope you do something about those crooks
— just as your pioneer ancestor did.”

00:02:14.909 --> 00:02:19.730
Movie studios were thinking about this too
—when Abbott and Costello met Frankenstein,

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:26.739
it joined the Dracula, Frankenstein, and Wolfman
movie franchises with a goofy comedy.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:33.709
It was basically Universal Studios’ Avengers
Infinity War, but with more running into walls.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:41.860
By the 60s, even DC comics was catching up,
like when Superman entered his fortress of

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.580
solitude and finally met Batman.

00:02:44.599 --> 00:02:47.240
“I love you!”

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:48.490
They enjoyed some cake.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.840
“You love me.”

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:57.719
By the time Spiderman got his comic in 1963,
he was spending time with the Fantastic Four

00:02:57.719 --> 00:03:00.680
and promoting guest appearances from the Hulk.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:05.580
And this was also when the Marvel Universe’s
problems started.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.560
This snowglobe contains a half-century of
television.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:17.719
It’s the basis for the Tommy Westphall Universe
theory, which some argue might be the biggest

00:03:17.719 --> 00:03:19.319
fictional universe of all.

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:23.530
The snowglobe appeared on the ‘80s television
show St. Elsewhere.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:28.950
That scene possibly showed that the whole
series had taken place inside a snowglobe

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:33.640
world imagined by
a minor character named Tommy Westphall.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:40.160
The theory is that if a show had a crossover
with St. Elsewhere, that show must have taken

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.950
place in Tommy’s snow globe too.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:48.870
And every crossover that show had, no matter
how tangential, would be in the snowglobe,

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:55.999
and so on and so on, until you get almost
450 television shows in the same universe,

00:03:55.999 --> 00:03:57.310
in the same snowglobe.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:01.740
It spans from 1952 to... today.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.040
It’s a dumb theory.

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:05.799
But, it’s meant to be.

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:10.959
That said, the Tommy Westphall universe actually
illustrates some of the problems with the

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.960
Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.520
Crossovers require a bunch of artistic compromises.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:22.760
No one wants Cheers and I Love Lucy to be
a connected “universe.”

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:27.440
It’s because of a bunch of business decisions
connected them.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:34.020
Sometimes, the network forced crossovers on
producers, other times, the producers came

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:40.180
up with it — Garry Marshall said the reason
that Mork from Ork knew Fonzie from Happy Days

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:46.380
and Laverne from Laverne and Shirley was because
his son said that Fonzie should dream about

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:47.380
an alien.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:51.420
These crossovers are always about juicing
ratings, not telling a good story.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.880
The Tommy Westphall universe is a thought
experiment.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.750
Frasier and The X-Files don’t mix, even
though they do in Tommy Westphall’s globe.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:04.040
But even at Marvel, where continuity was planned,
it became a hassle to maintain.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:10.360
Marvel editor and writer Len Wein said:
“The problem at Marvel was that we suddenly

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:15.080
became a business with a bunch of books that
Stan, [Lee] don’t think, ever in his heart

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.830
expected to last more than a couple of years.”

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:25.940
In 1965, Lee replaced most of the Avengers
just because the continuity for the original

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:29.070
group had become too confusing to deal with.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:32.210
This continuity clutter happened in the movies,
too.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.470
Iron Man kicked off the MCU in 2008.

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:39.520
The universe exposition dump didn’t happen
until after the credits.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:43.180
“Mr. Stark, you’ve become part of a bigger
universe.”

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:47.030
Ant Man required a weak excuse right in the
middle of the movie.

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:50.640
“I think our first move should be calling the Avengers."

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:57.180
“I spent half my life trying to keep this
technology out of the hands of Stark.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.770
I sure as hell am not gonna hand it to one
now.”

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:05.910
These heavy-handed additions are about getting
crossover hype, not about telling a good story.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:11.060
But there’s a bigger problem than continuity
in the MCU — and there is a way to do it

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:11.560
better.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:14.500
“Whoa you’re the Avengers.”

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:17.460
The rest of this scene is a standard movie
fight.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:23.010
Their universe is only as deep as merchandise
and a few Easter Eggs.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:28.380
It’s cool when Peter Parker’s classroom
has a picture of Bruce Banner in the corner.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:33.190
But it’s not a transformed universe,
It’s like Quentin Tarantino including the

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:37.500
imaginary brand of Red Apple cigarettes in
multiple movies.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:38.660
It’s cool.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.700
It’s not a coherent, intertwined world.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:49.260
The MCU is like our own, except for the crossovers. You can test it with the characters.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:50.980
Add Aquaman to that poster.

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:54.830
If you didn’t know DC owned him, would anyone
notice?

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:58.140
The MCU has no rules beyond corporate ownership.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.160
It's a superhero hodgepodge.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:03.030
The same goes for DC.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.860
If you added Hawkeye here, nobody would care.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:09.340
And not just because it’s...Hawkeye.

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:15.960
Marvel’s own property — X-Men, with a
movie universe owned by Fox and likely

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:19.270
going to Disney — shows a better way.

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:22.520
This universe is not just crossovers.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:29.140
Every character — human and mutant — has
taken sides in a generations-long battle,

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:32.920
with real stakes, unlike Marvel’s Civil
War.

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:39.860
That leads to creative possibilities, like
decade-jumping and even tonal experimentation

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:45.460
with deep integration to the X-Men mythology,
if not the continuity.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:51.380
When Marvel experiments, like with Jessica
Jones, the big tie in to the MCU is...merch.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:54.320
“Do you have any cool toys in your room
you want to show me?”

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:56.350
“Do you know Captain America?”

00:07:56.350 --> 00:08:01.700
X-Men movies are not all good, but they do
have a universal logic that’s stronger than

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:03.500
Tommy Westphall’s snowglobe.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:08.680
Here’s another test.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:13.590
If you — little old you — were in a movie,
which universe would change you?

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:17.840
In X-Men, you’d have to decide where you
fell in a decades-long battle.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:19.220
Do you side with mutants?

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:20.520
Or humans?

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:26.080
In the MCU, you’d be like this kid in Agents
of Shield — your big decision would be to

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:27.300
buy some merch.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:28.480
“I’m OK.”

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:35.840
Marvel has flirted with a richer universe,
one in which the lives of all people are transformed by

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:36.919
a shared history.

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:43.840
But right now, would anybody be surprised
if Disney forced a Marvel/Star Wars crossover?

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.390
Marvel and Disney can make the right choice.

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:55.770
In the late 80s, the Marvel comic Damage Control
toyed with the consequences of superhero life

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:57.680
on the world at large.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:04.580
That made its cameo in Spiderman: Homecoming
a thrilling indicator of where the MCU could

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:05.580
go.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:10.360
It was a world that anybody could imagine
themselves in.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:17.020
But until that experimentation transforms
the movies, the MCU is just a business strategy.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.480
The universe is still in development.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:29.881
If you want just the facts about Marvel, please
check out Sean Howe’s Marvel Comics: The

00:09:29.881 --> 00:09:31.550
Untold Story.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:37.640
It gives you a peek behind the scenes of Marvel
and how they shaped a half-century of our

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:38.140
culture.

